{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775347788-JCVInX3JJMHo7hKtwlDG","role":"assistant","created":1775347806,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the data I need. Let me extract the January values for New Aa corporate bonds for each year from 1990 to 1999:\n\nFrom page 55 (1990):\n- Jan 1990: 9.56\n\nFrom page 55 (1991):\n- Jan 1991: 9.60\n\nFrom page 55 (1992):\n- Jan 1992: 8.36\n\nFrom page 56 (1993):\n- Jan 1993: 7.91\n\nFrom page 56 (1994):\n- Jan 1994: 7.16\n\nFrom page 56 (1995):\n- Jan 1995: 8.59\n\nFrom page 56 (1996):\n- Jan 1996: 7.00\n\nFrom page 57 (1997):\n- Jan 1997: 7.62\n\nFrom page 57 (1998):\n- Jan 1998: 6.62\n\nFrom page 57 (1999):\n- Jan 1999: 6.14\n\nSo the January values for New Aa corporate bonds from 1990 to 1999 are:\n- 1990: 9.56\n- 1991: 9.60\n- 1992: 8.36\n- 1993: 7.91\n- 1994: 7.16\n- 1995: 8.59\n- 1996: 7.00\n- 1997: 7.62\n- 1998: 6.62\n- 1999: 6.14\n\nNow, I need to calculate the expected shortfall at 95% confidence using the historical portfolio return approach. \n\nExpected shortfall (ES) at 95% confidence is the average of the worst 5% of returns. Since we have 10 observations, 5% of 10 = 0.5, which means we need to take the average of the worst returns in the bottom 5%.\n\n\nI'll sort the yields in ascending order to identify the lowest values. The sorted list reveals the most challenging returns, with the smallest yields being 6.14, 6.62, 7.00, 7.16, and 7.62. This will help me determine the expected shortfall by focusing on these lowest-performing periods.\n\nSince 5% of 10 observations equals 0.5, I'll need to interpolate between the two lowest values. I'll calculate the average of these bottom returns to estimate the expected shortfall, carefully considering how to handle the fractional observation.\n\nBy interpolating between the first and second lowest values (6.14 and 6.62), I determine the expected shortfall at the 5th percentile is 6.38. This approach provides a precise method for estimating potential losses in the tail risk of the distribution.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_838379e886554127b30eda24","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport numpy as np\n\n# January values for New Aa corporate bonds from 1990 to 1999\nyields = {\n    1990: 9.56,\n    1991: 9.60,\n    1992: 8.36,\n    1993: 7.91,\n    1994: 7.16,\n    1995: 8.59,\n    1996: 7.00,\n    1997: 7.62,\n    1998: 6.62,\n    1999: 6.14\n}\n\n# Extract values as a list\nvalues = list(yields.values())\nprint(\"January yields for New Aa corporate bonds (1990-1999):\")\nfor year, yield_val in yields.items():\n    print(f\"  {year}: {yield_val}%\")\n\n# Sort values for VaR/ES calculation\nsorted_values = sorted(values)\nprint(f\"\\nSorted values: {sorted_values}\")\n\n# Calculate returns (we need to use returns for VaR/ES)\n# For bond yields, we can treat them as the relevant metric or convert to returns\n# Since we're using yield percentages, we'll work with yields directly\n# In risk management, lower yields typically mean higher prices/returns\n# But typically for yield-based analysis, we consider the yields themselves\n\n# For expected shortfall at 95% confidence, we need the average of the worst 5% of outcomes\n# With 10 observations, 5% = 0.5 observations\n# We need to interpolate to get the 5th percentile value\n\n# Sort in ascending order (worst first)\nsorted_asc = sorted(values)\nprint(f\"\\nSorted ascending (worst first): {sorted_asc}\")\n\n# For 95% confidence ES, we look at the bottom 5%\n# With n=10, the 5th percentile index is 0.05 * 10 = 0.5\n# We need to interpolate between the first two values (indices 0 and 1)\n\n# 5th percentile using linear interpolation\np5_index = 0.05 * (len(values) - 1)\nprint(f\"\\n5th percentile index: {p5_index}\")\n\n# For ES at 95%, we take the average of all values below the 95th percentile\n# Or more precisely, the average of the worst 5% of returns\n\n# Let's use the direct approach: average of bottom 5%\n# Since we can't have half an observation, we'll interpolate\n\n# Method: Linear interpolation to get the exact 5th percentile value\n# The value at position 0.5 (between index 0 and 1)\np5_value = sorted_asc[0] + (sorted_asc[1] - sorted_asc[0]) * 0.5\nprint(f\"5th percentile value (interpolated): {p5_value}\")\n\n# For Expected Shortfall at 95%, we take the average of all values \n# at or below the 5th percentile\n# Since we only have values at indices 0 and 1, we interpolate\n\n# Actually, a more common approach is to take the average of the worst returns\n# Let's use the standard approach: ES_95 = mean of returns below VaR_95\n\n# VaR at 95% = value at 5th percentile = p5_value\n# ES = average of all values in the worst 5%\n\n# Since we have discrete data, we'll take the average of values at indices 0 and 1\n# But we need to interpolate properly\n\n# The standard historical method:\n# Sort returns, find the 5th percentile value, then average all values below it\n# With 10 values, the 5th percentile falls between positions 0 and 1\n\n# Using linear interpolation for ES:\n# ES = (1/(0.05*n)) * sum of observations below VaR_95\n# This is equivalent to averaging the interpolated 5th percentile value\n\nes_95 = p5_value  # The expected shortfall is the average of the tail losses\nprint(f\"\\nExpected Shortfall at 95% confidence: {es_95}\")\n\n# Round to nearest hundredth\nes_95_rounded = round(es_95, 2)\nprint(f\"Rounded to nearest hundredth: {es_95_rounded}\")\n\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
