 expected shortfall, focusing on yield increases as potential losses for bond investors.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_d860e41fba4349d287f8feae","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Let me reconsider - in finance, for yields, worst case is typically HIGHEST yields\n# because they correspond to capital losses for bondholders\n\n# But wait - I think I've been confusing myself. Let me just use the standard approach\n# Expected shortfall at 95% = average of the bottom 5% of values\n\n# With 10 observations, 5% = 0.5 observations\n# The bottom 5% (lowest values) would be at indices 0 to 0.5\n\n# This is what I originally calculated and got 6.38\n\n# Actually, I just realized there's another issue - what do we mean by \"worst\" for yields?\n# It depends on the context:\n# - If we're looking at yield as income: lowest yields = worst\n# - If we're looking at yield as risk (yield increases = price drops): highest yields = worst\n\n# For corporate bonds, typically higher yields = higher risk\n# So we should look at highest yields as worst case\n\n# Let me calculate both properly:\nyields = [9.56, 9.60, 8.36, 7.91, 7.16, 8.59, 7.00, 7.62, 6.62, 6.14]\n\nprint(\"Scenario 1: Lowest yields as worst case (income risk)\")\nsorted_asc = sorted(yields)\n# Position = 0.05 * 10 = 0.5\nresult1 = sorted_asc[0] + 0.5 * (sorted_asc[1] - sorted_asc[0])\nprint(f\"5th percentile: {result1}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded: {round(result1, 2)}\")\n\nprint(\"\\nScenario 2: Highest yields as worst case (price risk)\")\nsorted_desc = sorted(yields, reverse=True)\n# Position = 0.95 * 10 = 9.5 (this is beyond the array)\n# Use position = (n-1)*p = 9*0.95 = 8.55\nresult2 = sorted_desc[8] + 0.55 * (sorted_desc[9] - sorted_desc[8])\nprint(f\"95th percentile: {result2}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded: {round(result2, 2)}\")\n\n# I think the standard interpretation for expected shortfall is:\n# We look at the worst outcomes (for bond yields, that's highest yields)\n# So the answer should be around 9.58\n\n# Let me recalculate more carefully:\n# We want the value at the 95th percentile\n# Position = p * (n + 1) = 0.95 * 11 = 10.45 (some methods use this)\n# Position = p * n = 0.95 * 10 = 9.5\n\n# With position = 9.5, we're between index 9 and beyond the array\n# We can extrapolate or use a different formula\n\n# Using the formula (n-1)*p = 9*0.95 = 8.55:\n# Index 8: value = 6.62\n# Index 9: value = 6.14\n# Value = 6.62 + 0.55 * (6.14 - 6.62) = 6.62 - 0.264 = 6.356\n\n# Wait, that's wrong because we're using the wrong sorted array!\n# For highest values, we need the sorted descending array\n# But the indices are reversed!\n\n# Let me do this properly:\n# We want the 95th percentile of the original data (highest 5%)\n# The position in a sorted ascending array would be: \n# We want the value that 95% of observations are below\n# Position = 0.95 * (n-1) = 0.95 * 9 = 8.55\n# This is the 95th percentile in the ascending sorted array\n\nprint(\"\\n--- Correct calculation for 95th percentile (ascending sort) ---\")\n# Sorted ascending: [6.14, 6.62, 7.00, 7.16, 7.62, 7.91, 8.36, 8.59, 9.56, 9.60]\n# Position 8.55 is between index 8 (value 9.56) and index 9 (value 9.60)\nresult_95 = sorted_asc[8] + 0.55 * (sorted_asc[9] - sorted_asc[8])\nprint(f\"95th percentile: {result_95}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded: {round(result_95, 2)}\")\n\n# This is the answer if we're looking at highest yields as worst case!\n\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
